	ARexx © Copyright 1987 William S. Hawes, Wishful Thinking
	Development Corp. All rights reserved.
@endnode

@node "Introduction, Preface"

	@{b}Introduction, Preface@{ub}
    
	Finally I've rewritten this 6 months old documentation and given
	it a new look that should be far more easier to read and get some
	hints from. This is revision V2.00 of the Account ED Package and
	you might have to take a closer look at some upgraded facilities
	that is not compatible with V1.05 - V1.03, but here we go from
	the beginning so hang on ..

	@{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} (Short name AccED) is a so called @{"ARexx" LINK "About ARexx"} Door for
	the /X 2.35+ and /X 3.00+ package and will interfere as an AIM
	(Arexx Interface Module). It will automatically detect which
	version you are running on and make the appropriate changes!

	If you use this door you can make the security on your board
	even higher with the high security features of this editor and
	accompanying doors. All these door is made out of a need which
	I got when I've been running my board! I hope they will fit yours.

	It's basically a replacement account editor for the old built in
	and it is really a big difference using this editor compared to
	the built in. Your life as a sysop will be much easier and looking
	at users accounts will be more fun. You will also get more control
	over your users and your system.

	The source is not compiled and should be rather easy for an ARexx
	programmer to take a look at, which you also should do if you
	want to be sure there is no backdoors provided with the source
	from one in the row of people who got it before you. To be sure
	wether there is a backdoor or not you should call my friend, Chucky's
        board called GANG BANG which is the official distribution site for
	my doors and my /X Echo Mail package which is soon available for a
	nominal fee. His board will serve as distribution site until I open
	my board again for the public! I'm right now doing a major update
	on the Echo Mail program so it can approximately be delivered first
	in late spring 1994. ßeta versions will be available sooner.

@endnode

@node "Features"

	@{b}Features of the Account Ed Package Doors@{ub}

	@{i}The Main Program@{ui}	@{i}The Accessory Doors@{ui}

	@{" Account ED    " LINK "Features of Account ED"}		@{" F6 Editor        " LINK "Features of F6 Account Editor"}
				@{" Quest            " LINK "Features of Quest"}
				@{" Quest View       " LINK "Features of Quest View"}
				@{" Password Fail    " LINK "Features of Password Fail"}
				@{" Security Checker " LINK "Features of Security Checker"}

	Note that these features are the most important, there exists
	many more than these features!

	To get MOST out of this package you should install ALL the above
	doors, however a minimum installation is possible with only
	Account ED and Quest. This will of course not give you all benefits!

@endnode

@node "Features of Security Checker"

	@{b}Security Level Checker@{ub}

	A litle tool which will help you against lamers who you by mistacke
	and/or has given a too high security level to sysop download something
	and then that lamer logs on and try to hack your board with 255 in
	security level.

	This tool will check all users who logs on and if they have a higher
	security level than the one you have specified in the AccEd.Presets
	file and they are not in the list of sysops you have in the AccEd.Preset
	file their security level will drop to the security level you have
	specified in the AccEd.Preset file.

	Their activity will also be logged in the AccEd.Log file, so you
	later on can see how tries to hack your board!

	This is an example from the log file output:

	04-08-94 Hack  Kalle Hackson         Too high security level (255) (15:06:19)

@endnode

@node "Features of F6 Account Editor"

	@{b}Features of F6 Account Editor@{ub}

	This @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} replaces the built in which appears when
	you press the F6 key inside AmiExpress. However it will for
	some reason not replace the account editor which appears when
	you are not logged on and presses F6. This is not the editors
	fault, it is caused by @{"AmiExpress" LINK "AmiExpress"}.

	In the SYSCmd directory or file I name the door Accounts, but
	if there is a name which lets you replace the editor which
	appears when you're not logged on just enter F6 Edit as the
	door there too, it should work fine in both cases.

	F6 @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} is exactly the same as Account ED, but VERY
	simplified with only the most common functions which you will
	need when a user is on-line. If you need more powerful functions
	use the real Account ED door instead! This door is only useful
	for upgrading users access and fix creds for them while they're
	on-line!

@endnode

@node "Features of Quest View"

	@{b}Features of Quest View@{ub}

	Quest View is simple a program for your users to peek at each
	others questionnaires. The sysop can decide which questions the
	user won't be shown and the user himself can decide if he should
	let the other users see this or not show his quest at all:

	Name
	Address
	Phonenumber

	These three items will of course only be available if the sysop
	has put these three questions in the questionary sheet.

	If the user wanted his personal info Public, Private or Secret
	will be	stored in the Account/QuestApp/X.Dat file where X is
	the users slot number.

	After displaying the users questionary, Quest View will also
	display some status about the user:

	Uploads/Downloads Bytes & Files
	UL/DL Ratio
	Number of Calls
	Number of written Messages
	BaudRate
	Last Time On-line
	Overall user performance ratio (Account ED Package Specific)

	This will give a clear picture of the user and could be useful
	for other sysops checking up a user who gave references to your
	board!!!

@endnode

@node "Features of Quest"

	@{b}Features of Quest@{ub}

	Quest lets you skip your Script14400 files and the Answers file
	which gives many sysops a hard headache.. I think you know
	what I mean when you've got 4 new users and then you should
	update them, phew what a boring job.. Load your answers file
	into CED and then go to the bottom of a 200kb large file!

	@{" In conjunction with Account ED " LINK "QuestED"}
	@{" Questionary Sheet              " LINK "Sheet"}
	@{" Letter                         " LINK "QuestLetter"}
	@{" Questioning old users          " LINK "OldQuest"}
	@{" Force answers                  " LINK "Force"}
	@{" Automatic Access               " LINK "Auto Access"}
	@{" Space saving                   " LINK "Space"}
	@{" Safety backups                 " LINK "Backups"}

@endnode

@node "QuestED"

	When running Account ED & Quest, which are the perfect couple..
	You get access to some notable features.

	Quest lets your users answer to all questions you prefer and
	then stores their answers separately in the Account/QuestApp
	directory. If you then want to look at any applications you just
	search for that user in the @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} and then press the / key
	and you will then immediately have that users application on your
	screen. If it concerns new user you just use the Scan for New Users
	function in the Account Editor and you will both see the users
	account and the questionary!! That's what I call powerful!
	You can even print the users questionary if you like to!

	All questions are of course configurable and you will in the
	future be able to both look at the users account and then
	also see some information displayed from the questionary!

@endnode

@node "Sheet"

	Quest has a questionary sheet in which you almost can program
	which sort of actions that should be taken.

	You could for instance tell that this question asks for the
	name of the user and then Account ED and Quest View could make
	use of it! You can enter lines that won't be asked if you have
	long questions and much more!

	For more information take a look in the installation chapter
	for the @{"Questionary Sheet" LINK "ConfigQuest"}!

@endnode

@node "QuestLetter"

	After the user has answered all the questions he will be forced
	to enter a presentation letter to the sysop or cosysop. The
	person who will receive the letter is configurable.

@endnode

@node "OldQuest"

	Suppose that you have installed quest and haven't been running
	it before, you now regret it and thinks that it would have
	been nice to view all your users questionnaires within the
	Account Editor, as you can do with Quest!

	The solution is to setup Quest as an MCI command in one of
	your login bulletins which all users can view.

	Just add this to the top of the bulletin: ~CC_QUEST

	This will invoke Quest every time this bulletin is shown, Quest
	then knows if a user has answered his questionary and Quest
	will only be started the first time the user gets the bulletin.

@endnode

@node "Force"

	Quest has a built in feature which forces the user to not leave
	any line blank. It could also be configured to kickout the user
	if he just presses return 5 times!

	All this features is of course configurable.

@endnode

@node "Auto Access"

	If you are a busy sysop you know that it's not much use of you
	looking for the users questionary and then giving them some lame
	access until you see that the user uploads. It would be much easier
	if the questionary program automatically gave the user lamer access
	if he just answers all questions!

	Quest checks if the user is neglecting the questions and if he don't
	the user will the access specified in the first preset! All presets
	are located in the AccEd.Presets file (together will all configs)

@endnode

@node "Space"

	Using Quest will save space on your harddrive because it only
	saves the answers and not the questions, which you already have
	in your questionary sheet.

	Quest takes the questions from your sheet and the answers from
	the users application and then displays it on your screen. This
	method gives you some extra features said in @{"Questionary Sheet" LINK "Sheet"}
	feature!

@endnode

@node "Backups"

	Once I got a virus on my Harddrive which wrote FUCK all over my
	harddrives block, so almost every file got a fuck within it!

	My user datas got totally (litterarily) FUCKED UP and my users
	wanted their credits back, they didn't have an account and so on!
	I didn't know their password (strange ehh?) and therefor it was
	impossible for me to do anything, but to delete the data files!

	The phone number and name and such thing however was left in my
	300 applications files, but without passwords...

	I therefor have put in a feature which posts the new users password
	in his questionary for use if an emergency situation would occur.

	But it would be a rather hardtime login on 300 users manually
	so Quest ALSO save the new users DATA and KEYS into one file
	which is called Slotnumber.DAT, for example 1.DAT. The *.Dat file
	also has got some flags for the user, if he want's to show his
	address and name to other users and such things.

	Account ED is then capable of loading in a backup of a users
	account anytime inside the @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"}, by pressing the SHIFT-B
	key! Every time you save a users account this .DAT file is also
	updated to keep it as fresh as possible for you!

	Could also be useful if you manage to fuckup a users account
	and wants the old account back!

@endnode

@node "Features of Password Fail"

	@{b}Features of Password Fail@{ub}

	Password Fail is a simple util which lets your users answers some
	short questions, write a letter to sysop and operate the sysop if
	the users fails to enter the correct password when logging on
	your system.

	@{" Questioning " LINK "Questioning"}
	@{" Letter      " LINK "Letter"}
	@{" Operate     " LINK "Operate"}
	@{" Log Files   " LINK "Logging"}

@endnode

@node "Questioning"

	When a user fails to enter his password three times, Pw Fail will
	be envoked and let your user answer some basic questions.

	Name
	Address
	Security Code
	Phone Number

	The password the user thought he had
	The new desired password
@endnode

@node "Letter"

	After filling out the basic questions, the user will have to write
	a letter to the sysop, with some presentation and tell something
	that makes the sysop certain that this is the real user.

	Together with the letter, PW Fail will post the questionary
	and the three password which the user tried to log on with.
	There will also be some additional information posted with the
	letter which PW Fail could find out.

	The letter will be posted to you or one of your cosysops, depending
	on which name you have written as the receiver in the config file.
@endnode

@node "Operate"

	After the user has filled out the letter, PW Fail gives him a chance
	to operate the sysop before dropping the carrier!
@endnode

@node "Logging"

	PW Fail will log all persons who fails with their passwords in
	a log file which is called PFail.log and is located in the Account/
	directory..

	If you have some users who frequently forget their passwords it's
	easy to see in the log file, and it only gives a clear picture if
	someone is trying to hack your board. A hacker who tries to cover
	his tracks, may also not be aware of this log file, which could
	give you some hint of who hacked your board, if someone does it!


	Example:

	Delusion       05-08-93 17:52:15 PW: (vf44kllk) (vf43fdfg) (vd£2fggg)
	Delusion       05-08-93 17:55:47 PW: (v7dj32kl) (vfs3w456) (vd113454)


	Here it's easy to see what users have tried, even if you have a
	callerslog cutter, all users mistyped passwords will be preserved
	and you can easy see a pattern if someone tries to hack your board!

	Maybe I'll make a TOP PASSWORD forgetters util which can be used
	together with this logfile later on :)

@endnode

@node "Features of Account ED"

	@{b}Features of Account ED@{ub}

	Account ED is designed with high security in mind and I think my
	door is the first one that can lock out a user at a specific time
	period, ie between 24.00 and 08.00 in the morning if you think
	that no one should access the @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} then. Below this text
	follows most of the features available in Account ED. Enjoy!


            @{" New features            " LINK "NewFeatures"}   @{" Print account           " LINK "PrintAccount"}
            @{" Name checking           " LINK "NameCheck"}   @{" Print questionnaires     " LINK "PrintQuest"}
            @{" Password checking       " LINK "PasswordCheck"}   @{" Quest compatibillity    " LINK "QuestCompatible"}
            @{" Restrict commands       " LINK "RestrictCommands"}   @{" Messagebase cleaner     " LINK "MsgBaseCleaner"}
            @{" Conference Access       " LINK "ConferenceAccess"}   @{" Lock users passwords    " LINK "LockPassword"}
            @{" Userlist tool           " LINK "UserlistTool"}   @{" Security conference     " LINK "SecurityConference"}
            @{" Write warning note      " LINK "WarningNote"}   @{" Built in calculator     " LINK "Calculator"}
            @{" Credit Maintenance      " LINK "Credit"}   @{" Compatibility!!         " LINK "Compatibility"}
            @{" Credited Users List     " LINK "CreditList"}   @{" Activity reports        " LINK "LogFiles"}


	These are more features than many doors together can't provide you
	with, so you'd better consider to take a closer look or you'll
	have to regret it! :)

	Note! AccountED will autodetect which /X version you are running
	      and reconfigure itself for your version! (2 programs in 1)
@endnode

@node "CreditList"

	@{b}Credit UserList Creator@{ub}

	For AmiExpress users 3.37 and over you can use the built in Credit
	UserList Creator. This will enable you to see the total amount of
	money paid to your BBS and the total amount of money paid the last
	time to your board.

	This is how the output could look:

	1 Delusion    oo Virtual oo 255 240338912 567 31 Mar 94 06 Nov 94 1234

	Slot            Security Level  Downloads  |    Start      End    Total
                                                   |                      Paid
                                           Paid Last

	This will give you a pretty clear overview of your credited users!

@endnode

@node "Credit"

	@{b}Credit Maintenance Editor@{ub}

	From version 3.37 of AmiExpress there is a Credit Maintenance Editor
	included in the internal Account Editor. This one is 100% compatible,
	but has the same advantages as the AccountED like calculator and
	wordprocessor-like editing capabilities!

	For you with older versions of AmiExpress this feature will disappear
	because crediting will only be useful if your system supports it!

	What is crediting? Crediting keeps track on how much a user has paid
	for Free Download in a certain period and also removes the Free DL
	after that period. It also keep track on the total amount of money
	that user has paid to the system!

	NOTE! Account ED's credit util will create a CreditLog file which
	      is NOT compatible with the built in (which doesn't affect
	      anything), but it's MUCH MORE easier to read and doesn't
	      look like shit when you want to look at it (like the built in)!
@endnode

@node "Compatibility"

	@{b}Account ED's enormous Compatibility@{ub}

	This is the most compatible door of this class I know. It will work
	on the following configurations:

	AmiExpress 2.0 and higher
	AmiExpress 3.0 and higher

	and even AmiExpress 3.37 and higher

	These versions really differs in the way of handling the user data.
	Version 2.0 to 3.0 does only have 9 conferences and you mark them
	with a X or _ depending on what access you give them.

	Version 3.0 to 3.36 has a scrambled password for each user and also
	uses unlimited conferences and therefore has got a name instead
	of X and _:s, these show what conferences the user got access to.

	Somewhere between version 3.20 and 3.37 Chat limit time and Chat
	used time was added to the user data. Of course this has been added
	to Account ED too, and it only works from 3.20 and higher!

	Credit Maintenance util was introduced in AmiExpress 3.37 and
	to keep compatibility I included one in Account ED too. This
	utility can't be used by other /X versions.

	NOTE! If you have an older /X you won't notice all new features
	      because they are hidden for you. If you upgrade your /X
	      you can immediately take advantage of them :)

@endnode

@node "NameCheck"

	@{b}User Name Checking@{ub}

	It checks if a person got the right name from a list of
	persons allowed to use the @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"}. This works in the same
	way as Maltese Falcons Remote Shell and Sysop Download utils.
@endnode

@node "PasswordCheck"

	@{b}User Password Checking@{ub}

	This function is configurable so if you don't want it, it won't
	ask for a password. Some want it so it's the only reason why it
	exists. If you enter a password instead of the blanks in the
	AccED.Preset file it will ask for the password. The letters you
	type will be replaced with *'s!
@endnode

@node "LogFiles"

	@{b}Activity Reports@{ub}

	Every entry in the user data that someone who is in Account ED
	look at will be reported and logged in a special Account ED log
	file. It will also add comments if someone read or wrote an account
	and if you print an account.
@endnode

@node "PrintAccount"

	@{b}Print User Account@{ub}

	Yes, you could actually print an account to your printer using the
	printer settings from your Amiga DOS.
	This feature is really my favorite, if you are going to delete a
	user you could easily print the users account before selecting
	delete and store it somewhere for no special reason ..
@endnode

@node "PrintQuest"

	@{b}Print User Questionary@{ub}

	Printing a questionary works exactly the same as printing an
	account, thus you got to have the Quest Package too (available
	at INSANiTY, if your local boards can't provide you)
@endnode

@node "RestrictCommands"

	@{b}Restrict Certain Commands@{ub}

	You can lock certain functions in the @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} for your
	cosysops so they only could use the 6 pre defined presets, no
	I'm joking .. 6 presets, NO! the 9 pre defined presets with a
	comment which you can write so the cosysop knows what each preset
	stands for and for which users they are meant. Nice ehh!?
@endnode

@node "ConferenceAccess"

	@{b}Give/Remove Conference Accesses@{ub}

	Like the MCP (Master Control Program) by Bryce, you can give and
	take access for a conference for all your users, so if you close
	a conference you easily can delete all users access to it or if
	you simply have started a new area. But.. wouldn't it be nice to
	go a little bit further and include one more nice feature into that
	powerful command? Yes! You can define between which two access
	levels a user must be so you could give/take access from users who
	only has got the security level 30 to 40 i.e..
@endnode

@node "UserlistTool"

	@{b}Full UserList Tool@{ub}

	The need for a powerful and fast userlist creator has now been
	fulfilled. This userlist creator can create any userlist you
	want and also save it as a file.

	- Do you want a plain userlist with all user who has security level
	  200 to 255, no problem.

	- Do you want a userlist with all users who has uploaded 2 megabytes
	  and downloaded maximum 30 megabytes and has got access to
	  conference 4 ??? NO PROBLEM

	- What about a look over the ul/dl stats for all users with disabled?

	- Perhaps all members to a certain area..

	- Or what about.. All users that has security level 50 to 80, has
	  uploaded minimum 10 mb and maximum 50 mb, has downloaded min 30 mb
	  and max 100 mb and also has got access to area 6! Can your userlist
	  maker do THAT? Well I doubt that anyway..

	There are no limits in this *VERY* sophisticated userlist tool
	which handles all of the above and more with just one routine!

	All this comes with a very nice ANSI output and some stats about
	how many percent of the users you've listed and so on..
@endnode

@node "NewFeatures"

	@{b}The New Features@{ub}

	Back to the main @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} there are some major line ups if
	you compare to the built in editor. First of all the design is
	made to be easy to overview and fast to get some particular
	information about one user. The built in one was so messy that I
	had a hard time to understand my own account with the download /
	upload cps and location put like a joke on the screen. This is
	should be easy to read and each function has got new letters
	because the old ones was worn out and not well placed. I hope
	you'll soon learn the new keys!
@endnode

@node "HelpPage"

	@{b}Help Page@{ub}

	The help page inside the @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} is for easy access to
	the most common functions inside the editor. You simple press
	a key and you can view it at the same time as the account is on
	the same page so you easily could compare and so on.
@endnode

@node "QuestCompatible"

	@{b}Quest Comatibillity@{ub}

	If you have used my Questionary Door called Quest (last version
	V1.09) this program is fully compatible with Quest. Anytime when
	you are reading a persons account you could press a key and get
	that users questionary on your screen. Very useful if you upgrade
	first time users.. I think you remember yourself loading a text-
	editor with your Answers file and trying to upgrade that poor
	users account, but how did your cosysops manage!? I guess that
	they didn't and the security with new users failed on your board!
@endnode

@node "WarningNote"

	@{b}Give User a Warning Note@{ub}

	Ever wanted a function that writes a letter very fast to your
	users and saying that they should upload and shows the users
	stats with uploaded/download bytes & file, calls and messages?
	Well it's built in into Account ED!

	You don't have to write a single line, just press a key..
@endnode

@node "MsgBaseCleaner"

	@{b}Message Base Cleaner Tool@{ub}

	A thing that can make your nerves pop up is when you have
	to delete old received messages from users who has not choosen
	delete then they've replied letters and all those EALL messages
	that your new users hate.. Account ED has a built in function
	which scans your msgbase and deletes old mail and asks what
	you want to do with the EALL messages!!
@endnode

@node "LockPassword"

	@{b}Show/Remove User Password@{ub}

	You can give a special access level for viewing the passwords
	of all accounts, if you do not have this security level Account ED
	will prompt Not Displayed as a password. You can choose if the
	password default should be shown to the ones with the security
	level or they have to press a special key combination for the
	password to appear. Useful if you have some friends beside you!
@endnode

@node "SecurityConference"

	@{b}High Security Conference@{ub}

	What about security for your groups conference then? Well as this
	program has been around a while I've thought of that too, so you
	could specify a conference to which no one could give access!
	This means that your cosysop can not give himself access to your
	groups member area!!
@endnode

@node "Calculator"

	@{b}Full Built-in Calculator@{ub}

	Ok I see that you are beginning to understand why I developed
	this new @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"}, so I will now give you only one more
	reason to use it. If you sometimes have an upload competition on
	your board you know how annoying it is to give that poor winner
	his rewarded upload credits, it usually means that you have
	to run down the stairs to get a pocket calculator and then
	enter his new upload bytes. But you could stop doing that
	already today if you use this account editor with built in
	calculator which can add and sub from a users account. You just
	choose the field you want to change and use the upper case of
	the letter instead and you will be asked if you want to sub or
	add and how many bytes. This works for all fields that has got
	to do with upload, download and limits.
@endnode

@node "FieldEditor"

	@{b}The New Field Editor@{ub}

	Oh, sorry I forgot to tell you one thing. In the /X account editor
	when you choose i.e. to change the users uploaded creds it clears
	that field and you have to try to remember what there was standing.
	My @{"Account Editor" LINK "About Account ED"} will not remove anything it works just like
	any professional editor like Cygnus ED. You write something and
	the text before your cursor will move to the right. If you use
	backspace the text to the light of the cursor will move to the left!
	The field that you may use for that specific item in the editor
	is also highlighted in blue, to give you a better overview!

	Of course the text that stood there before will be there until you
	use your DEL or BACKSPACE key to remove it.
@endnode

@node "Installation 2.10+ .."

	@{b}Installation on a based /X 2.10+ System ..@{ub}

	@{" File installation          " LINK "Install2.10+"}
	@{" Info about the Preset file " LINK "Preset Info!"}
	@{" Preset file configuration  " LINK "Presets2.10+"}
	@{" Questionary configuration  " LINK "ConfigQuest"}
	@{" Upgrade notes              " LINK "Upgrade"}

	It's recommended to make a backup of you critical files before
	running this door if you want to be 100% sure so it doesn't damage
	anything. The door has worked in most environments, but the
	developers of @{"AmiExpress" LINK "AmiExpress"} rapidly changes their data file
	structures and that may cause failure on your system.

	Backup this:

	BBS:User.Data
	BBS:User.Keys

	BBS:Conf{x}/MsgBase/HeadFile
	BBS:Conf{x}/MsgBase/MailStats

	These are the only files that the Account ED Package touches!
@endnode

@node "Installation 3.00+ .."

	@{b}Installation on a based /X 3.00+ System ..@{ub}

	@{" File installation          " LINK "Install3.00+"}
	@{" Info about the Preset file " LINK "Preset Info!"}
	@{" Preset file configuration  " LINK "Presets3.00+"}
	@{" Questionary configuration  " LINK "ConfigQuest"}
	@{" Upgrade notes              " LINK "Upgrade"}

	It's recommended to make a backup of you critical files before
	running this door if you want to be 100% sure so it doesn't damage
	anything. The door has worked in most environments, but the
	developers of @{"AmiExpress" LINK "AmiExpress"} rapidly changes their data file
	structures and that may cause failure on your system.

	Backup this:

	BBS:User.Data
	BBS:User.Keys

	BBS:Conf{x}/MsgBase/HeadFile
	BBS:Conf{x}/MsgBase/MailStats

	These are the only files that the Account ED Package touches!
@endnode

@node "Preset Info!"

	@{b}Information About The Preset File@{ub}

	The preset file is the most important file for Account ED.
	Without it shouldn't work. It's also important that
	you update your preset file at the same time as you update
	your Account ED Version.

	The preset file consist of many configurations for:

	Account ED	- The Account Editor
	F6 Editor	- Same as Account ED, but for online use
	Quest		- New User Questionary Program
	Quest View	- Questionary Viewer for Users
	Password Fail	- If a user has forgotten the password

	You do only have to configure the settings for the programs
	you have installed. All other data will be ignored by AccED.

	For a hacker the AccEd.Preset file should be of high priority
	so should restrict that file in every door you have and in
	your Acp.StartUp file.
@endnode

@node "Upgrade"

	@{b}Upgrading from older versions of Account ED@{ub}

	You have to completely rewrite you AccED.Preset file if you
	are upgrading from < V1.03. Upgrading from V1.04-1.05 will only
	require slightly adjustments.

	Previous user must note that there should be 3 new directories inside
	the Account/ directory called: QuestApp, QuestDel and QuestQst.
	if you are previous running Quest and has got it in the
	Doors:Arexx/Quest/ dir you should move your questions from the answers
	dir to the QuestApp dir in the Account directory. Otherwise Account ED
	can't read the questionaries! (More info in the Quest doc file)

	The questionary files for Quest has been changed!! You now has to add
	one more item before every question. Before it stood ~NQuestion, now
	it should stand ~N!Question the ! character marks if the question
	should be showed, when a user uses the Quest View program to look
	up a user, in this way a sysop can protect some of the secret info
	that he doesn't want the other users to know about each others!

	In previous preset files (V1.03) the computer list was in this form:
	Amiga 500¬Amiga 1000¬ and so on, now it should stand in descending
	order in the column which says computers.

	Under the names with the persons who has got access to Account
	(Delusion Fox 'N' Philix D-Zire) is all the configuration datas for
        both Quest and Account ED, all values who has got to do with Quest is
	explained separately in the Quest Doc.
@endnode

@node "ConfigQuest"

	@{b}Configuration of the Questionary Sheet@{ub}

	Old users of Quest should also read this, it's completely different!

	As you may be familiar to the MCI commands inside /X I've created
	something that looks the same, but in a reverse mode if you could
	say so. Before every question you can give these command:

	~	- Just question that question
	        - Nothing, means it's a comment and will only be displayed
	~N	- It tells Account ED that the users real name is asked
	~A	- Tells that it's the users address being asked, note that
		  you can use this command unlimited times in your quest
	~C	- Tells that you are asking for the users country
	~V	- Tells that you are asking for the users Voice Phone Number
	~D	- Tells that you are asking for the users Data  Phone Number
	~S	- Tells that you are asking for the users Sex & Age
	~P	- Tells that you are asking for the users Social Security Code
	~U	- Tells that you are asking for what the users does with his
		  computer and such things.
	~W	- Tells that you are asking for from where he got you phone #
	~R	- Tells that this is a question with the reasons why you should
		  let that user in. You can use this command unlimited times
		  to give the users more than one line to answer on!
	~E	- Just like the ~A and ~R command, but this is for the users
		  presentation.

	~ !	- The ! command means that this question won't be displayed
		  then a user is looking at another users questionary with
		  the Quest View program. This is useful if the sysop doesn't
		  want to share all information in the questionnaires with
		  the users!!

	Example:

	~N!What is your name ? :

	This asks the user of his name, and other users can not look at his
	name through the Quest View program, of course is it viewable for the
	sysop and cosysops!

	~  What do you think about Coca Cola ? :

	This just asks the question!

	   --------- Personal Info -----------

	This will just display this text! Note the 3 Spaces before the actual
	text starts! This is the command for displaying a text!

	For your comfort n example questionary has been included. The quest
	is originally designed by The Silencer / TRSI, but slightly remade
	by Delusion and of course improved!

	Anyway you now might wonder why you should tell the Questionary program
	what you intend to ask and such things, well it's very simple!
	In coming versions of Account ED it should be possible to both get
	the users Account and the most essential information from the questionary
	on the screen at the same time, like the users name and social security
	code. I have NOT coded that feature in Account ED yet, but it will
	very soon come. So here is your chance to be one step ahead :) so you
	will have it working as soon as the new Account ED arrives.

	However Quest View make use of these letter combinations to keep track
	on what to display or not. A user could choose to not show his addy
	and phone number to other users, and therefor Quest View need to know
	what is the name and so on.

	In older versions of Quest you could choose to save the users answers
	with questions and answers or answers alone. Because of the above said
	new features the questions and answers format doesn't work so therefor
	that function has been removed and you can only save the answers alone.

	Account ED still supports both formats so you can view older
	questionaries, but you have to the ~X commands to your old questionaries
	if you have some in your Doors:Arexx/Quest/Questions directory and copy
	them into the Doors:Arexx/Account/QuestQST dir. As you may have guessed
	the function with displaying info in Account Ed will not work on old
	questionnaires which is in the form Questions - Answers. I will maybe
	fix a converter util for that.

	Note that all commands has to be three chars, if you only have a ~ on
	a question it should look like "~  " and if you have a two chars command
	it should look like "~N " and for the three character command you should
	write "~N!".

	In otherwords this means that all questions should start on column 4
	and in column 1, 2 and 3 there should be the commands!
@endnode

